;; Hand-owned derived assets and their staleness tripwire (ADR-0158,
;; review-4 register row D5-2, author ruling R4-Q5 (b)).
;;
;; Five `docs/manual/assets/*.svg` each carry a banner naming a SPECIFIC
;; live source and the trigger that invalidates the drawing. No generator
;; exists for them, so they cannot join `docsgen`; what they can have is
;; a tripwire. `ehrt.docs-tooling.hand-owned-asset-freshness-test` reads
;; this file and asserts, per row, that the source's own last-change
;; commit is still `:reviewed-at`.
;;
;; :reviewed-at is a WITNESS, not a timestamp: it records the source
;; commit a human last looked at while holding the asset beside it. A
;; whole-file tripwire is deliberately coarser than the banners' own
;; section-level triggers, so it fires on edits that do not actually
;; invalidate the drawing. That is not a defect to be tuned away -- it is
;; why the disposition is a REVIEW. Clearing a red means opening the diff,
;; deciding whether the cited trigger fired, and then either bumping
;; :reviewed-at (with :note saying what moved and why it did not matter)
;; or marking :verdict :stale.
;;
;; :verdict is :fresh or :stale. A :stale row is a rowed FINDING, never a
;; silencer: it must name a live `roadmap.md` anchor in :stale-row, and
;; the test asserts that anchor still exists. Deleting the roadmap row
;; turns the suite red rather than quietly retiring the finding.
;;
;; Population is the TREE (`rulings.md#R-population-closure`): the test
;; globs `docs/manual/assets/*.svg` and requires a row for each, so a
;; sixth asset cannot ship un-tripwired.

[{:asset "docs/manual/assets/gt-emitters.svg"
  :source "docs/dev/simulator-architecture.md"
  :trigger "regenerate by hand if simulator-architecture.md section 4's own equations change"
  :reviewed-at "9f7697a8"
  :verdict :fresh
  :witness {:adr "ADR-0120" :date "2026-08-12"}}

